Title:
Hell’s Gate, Heaven’s Gate & Tartarus Gate

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This post explains three syscall-based EDR evasion techniques that aim to bypass user-mode API hooking: Hell’s Gate, Heaven’s Gate, and Tartarus Gate.  
- It focuses on how attackers can reach the kernel via direct/indirect syscalls or architecture transitions, avoiding instrumented user-mode ntdll paths.  
- Hell’s Gate dynamically extracts System Service Numbers (SSNs) from ntdll stubs at runtime to avoid hardcoded syscall IDs; Heaven’s Gate uses WoW64 32→64-bit transitions to call the less-hooked 64-bit layer; Tartarus Gate (building on HalosGate) recovers SSNs even when stubs are patched by scanning for clean neighboring stubs and compensating offsets.  
- The article also highlights defensive implications: detecting modified ntdll stubs (e.g., JMP 0xE9 trampolines), syscall-pattern heuristics, call stack anomalies from non-ntdll syscall origins, and reliance on kernel telemetry like ETW-TI.  
- Useful for red teamers and malware researchers to understand bypass mechanics, and for blue teams/detection engineers to build resilient detections beyond user-mode hooks.  
- It’s interesting because it frames the “Gate” techniques as an evolution responding to increasingly aggressive EDR hooking and shows where kernel-level visibility remains a backstop.

Technical Focus:
- Direct syscalls and dynamic SSN extraction from ntdll stubs (Hell’s Gate)
- WoW64 / 32-bit to 64-bit execution transitions (Heaven’s Gate)
- Hook detection via stub pattern checks (e.g., JMP 0xE9) and stub-walking heuristics (Tartarus Gate / HalosGate lineage)
- ntdll in-memory vs on-disk prologue integrity and trampoline detection
- ETW Threat Intelligence (ETW-TI) and kernel telemetry as hook-bypass-resistant visibility
- Call stack validation / anomalous syscall origin detection

Use Cases:
- Assessing how user-mode API hooking can be bypassed via syscall techniques
- Building or reviewing syscall-resolution logic resilient to Windows version SSN changes
- Developing detections for ntdll stub tampering and trampoline-based hooks
- Designing defensive coverage that correlates user-mode hook visibility with ETW-TI/kernel events
- Threat research and reverse engineering of malware using “Gate” syscall patterns

Keywords:
Hell’s Gate, Heaven’s Gate, Tartarus Gate, HalosGate, direct syscall, indirect syscall, SSN, System Service Number, ntdll.dll, user-mode hooking, EDR evasion, WoW64, 32-bit to 64-bit transition, syscall stub, JMP 0xE9, trampoline hook, ETW-TI, call stack validation, NtAllocateVirtualMemory, NtWriteVirtualMemory